WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.520
Begin with the bean type external view.

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:04.980
A points to the protective seed coat.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:09.960
B identifies the hilum, the scar left by the former attachment to the funicle.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:15.602
C marks the smaller micropyle near it, a persistent opening associated with

00:00:15.602 --> 00:00:15.800
the

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:16.560
ovule integuments.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.380
A scar is not the same as a pore.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.340
The seed coat commonly has an outer testa and an inner tegmen, but this

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:27.340
external outline does not resolve those layers separately.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.820
Seed shape and coat details vary among plants.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.780
Do not infer the exact internal axis position or the presence of mature

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:38.500
endosperm from this selected surface drawing alone.

00:00:38.712 --> 00:00:41.772
Add internal context without inventing a section.

00:00:42.472 --> 00:00:46.252
In familiar mature bean type seeds, much of the reserve food is in the

00:00:46.252 --> 00:00:49.432
cotyledons and the endosperm has been used during development.

00:00:50.052 --> 00:00:55.052
This does not mean every dicot seed lacks retained endosperm; castor is a

00:00:55.052 --> 00:00:56.612
familiar endospermous dicot example.

00:00:57.332 --> 00:01:00.912
Endosperm originates through the fertilisation pathway involving the central

00:01:00.912 --> 00:01:04.012
cell, whereas persistent nucellus is called perisperm.

00:01:04.012 --> 00:01:08.852
Both terms concern tissue identity and developmental origin, not just where

00:01:08.852 --> 00:01:09.472
food is stored.

00:01:10.072 --> 00:01:12.652
The embryo and seed coat also have different origins.

00:01:13.312 --> 00:01:17.672
A seed diagram should be read using its example and maturity stage rather than

00:01:17.672 --> 00:01:20.532
a universal dicot versus monocot food storage rule.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.040
The second board spreads out a selected dicot embryo.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.620
A marks the cotyledons, with a corresponding cotyledon on the opposite side.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.140
B points to the plumule, the embryonic shoot region.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:37.080
C identifies the epicotyl, the portion of the embryonal axis above the

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:38.140
cotyledon attachment level.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:43.107
The horizontal dashed line is a reference for that level, not an extra organ

00:01:43.107 --> 00:01:43.220
or

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:44.160
a cut through a vessel.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:48.860
Cotyledons are part of the embryo, not the seed coat and not endosperm.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.680
Their size and function vary among species.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:56.840
This simplified spread view exposes relationships that an intact external seed

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:57.840
drawing cannot show.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.020
D marks the hypocotyl, below the cotyledon attachment level.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:06.740
E points to the terminal radicle, the embryonic root region.

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:12.680
Do not call the entire lower axis a radicle or confuse the hypocotyl with the

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.360
epicotyl above the attachment level.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:19.800
During ordinary germination the radicle develops into the primary root, while

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.440
the plumule contributes to the shoot.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:26.760
The drawing shows a selected embryo before seedling emergence, not the full

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.160
sequence of epigeal or hypogeal germination.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:33.680
A root emerging first does not prove that every seed follows an identical

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:34.280
timetable.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:38.920
Use positions relative to the cotyledonary node to interpret the labels.

00:02:39.144 --> 00:02:45.104
The maize longitudinal section is a grain, or caryopsis: a one seeded

00:02:45.104 --> 00:02:48.064
fruit with its pericarp closely fused to the seed coat.

00:02:49.124 --> 00:02:51.264
A marks that combined outer region.

00:02:52.184 --> 00:02:56.824
B locates the peripheral aleurone region of the retained endosperm, while C

00:02:56.824 --> 00:02:59.164
marks the large starchy endosperm region.

00:02:59.164 --> 00:03:02.524
Aleurone is not an extra maternal seed coat.

00:03:03.144 --> 00:03:07.004
The outlines locate tissue regions and do not resolve every cell layer.

00:03:07.504 --> 00:03:12.004
This grain retains substantial endosperm at maturity, unlike the familiar bean

00:03:12.004 --> 00:03:13.404
type example discussed earlier.

00:03:14.044 --> 00:03:18.224
The comparison concerns the selected examples, not a rule that every monocot

00:03:18.224 --> 00:03:19.944
must have an identical reserve pattern.

00:03:20.208 --> 00:03:24.868
D identifies the shield like scutellum on the embryo side of the maize grain.

00:03:25.508 --> 00:03:29.488
It is the single cotyledon of the grass embryo, not a separate piece of

00:03:29.488 --> 00:03:29.848
endosperm.

00:03:30.948 --> 00:03:33.928
E locates the simplified embryonal axis.

00:03:35.008 --> 00:03:37.868
Together, scutellum and axis belong to the embryo.

00:03:38.148 --> 00:03:42.448
The scutellum assists transfer of reserves from endosperm during germination,

00:03:43.088 --> 00:03:45.528
but that role does not change its embryonic identity.

00:03:46.198 --> 00:03:50.848
The large starchy region at C is endosperm rather than a giant

00:03:50.848 --> 00:03:52.748
cotyledon.

00:03:52.748 --> 00:03:57.008
Position, developmental origin and connection with the axis are stronger clues

00:03:57.008 --> 00:03:58.308
than relative size alone.

00:03:59.228 --> 00:04:02.408
The next board enlarges the axis and protective sheaths.

00:04:02.616 --> 00:04:08.516
The enlarged grass embryo labels A as scutellum, B as coleoptile and C as

00:04:08.516 --> 00:04:08.756
plumule.

00:04:09.356 --> 00:04:13.343
The coleoptile is the protective sheath enclosing the young shoot region,

00:04:13.343 --> 00:04:13.616
while

00:04:13.616 --> 00:04:15.736
the plumule is the developing shoot within it.

00:04:16.316 --> 00:04:18.636
They are not alternative names for the same structure.

00:04:19.536 --> 00:04:23.216
A visible enclosing outline should therefore be traced separately from the

00:04:23.216 --> 00:04:23.856
structure it protects.

00:04:24.456 --> 00:04:28.956
The scutellum remains the single cotyledon; neither protective sheath adds

00:04:28.956 --> 00:04:29.836
another cotyledon.

00:04:30.536 --> 00:04:34.691
Endosperm and the outer grain coverings are omitted in this enlargement so

00:04:34.691 --> 00:04:34.916
that

00:04:34.916 --> 00:04:36.396
the axis can be read clearly.

00:04:37.316 --> 00:04:40.156
Do not identify the enlargement as an intact maize grain.

00:04:40.368 --> 00:04:45.908
D identifies the radicle and E the surrounding coleorhiza at the lower end of

00:04:45.908 --> 00:04:46.828
the grass embryo.

00:04:46.828 --> 00:04:51.548
Coleorhiza protects the root region; coleoptile protects the shoot region

00:04:51.548 --> 00:04:51.928
above.

00:04:52.928 --> 00:04:56.648
Pair each sheath with the structure inside it rather than memorising two

00:04:56.648 --> 00:04:58.548
similar sounding names without positions.

00:04:59.428 --> 00:05:03.748
The axis links shoot and root regions, and the scutellum attaches laterally.

00:05:04.488 --> 00:05:09.248
This is a selected anatomical model, not a timed germination experiment or

00:05:09.248 --> 00:05:12.688
evidence that every monocot embryo has exactly the same proportions.

00:05:14.408 --> 00:05:18.734
Finish by distinguishing coverings, reserve tissue, cotyledons and growing

00:05:18.734 --> 00:05:18.968
axis

00:05:18.968 --> 00:05:19.388
regions.

00:05:20.188 --> 00:05:23.548
Those categories prevent many common errors in seed label questions.
